.TH NEXMESH 1 "November 2026" "nexmesh 0.1.0" "Fleet control"
.SH NAME
nexmesh \- drive a team of agent CLIs across every machine you own
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nexmesh
.RI [ global-options ]
.I command
.RI [ arguments ]
.br
.B nexmesh
.RI [ global-options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nexmesh
is the operator's command line for a nexmesh fleet. It talks to the
.BR nexmesh-hub (1)
over HTTPS; the hub holds all state and is the only component that does.
.PP
Four things are easy to confuse, and nearly every argument below is one of
them. They are, from smallest to largest:
.TP
.B conversation
One window of one agent CLI \(em one
.BR claude ,
.B codex
or
.B opencode
session. Its own id is what makes a seat the same seat across a restart.
You never name a conversation in a command.
.TP
.B seat
A place on a team, with a stable name, that outlives whatever process is
filling it. Written
.I sl-1a2b3c4d
internally; addressed by its
.B name
\(em what the AGENT column of the dashboard shows, and what
.B status
prints. This is what commands mean by
.IR agent " or " who .
.TP
.B session
A team: one workspace, one message log, one lock domain, one token budget.
Addressed by
.B name
or by id
.RI ( se-1a2b3c4d ).
Many conversations belong to one session.
.TP
.B node
A machine running
.BR nexmesh-node (1).
Addressed by the name it enrolled under \(em
.IR machine-a ", " machine-b .
.PP
So in
.PP
.RS
.B nexmesh attach payments Reviewer
.RE
.PP
the words are: the command, the
.B session
called
.IR payments ,
and the
.B seat
called
.IR Reviewer .
A session named after the tool itself reads oddly \(em
.B nexmesh attach mesh Mesh-dev
is command, session, seat \(em but the positions never change, and a session
can be renamed at any time with
.BR rename-session .
.SH FINDING A SEAT
Most commands that act on one agent want its
.BR seat ,
and every one of them means the same thing: the
.B SEAT
column of
.BR "nexmesh status" .
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ nexmesh status payments
NAME          SEAT          CLI       ROLE       HOST       KIND      STATE
Reviewer      sl-1a2b3c4d   codex     reviewer   machine-b  attached  idle
Builder       sl-9f8e7d6c   claude    engineer   machine-a  managed   working
.fi
.RE
.PP
So
.I sl-1a2b3c4d
is what
.BR spawn ", " move-agent ", " remove-agent
and
.B rename-agent
take, and
.I Reviewer
is what
.BR attach ", " send " \(em\& " --to ", " pause ", " resume
and
.B release
take. Where a command accepts either, its help says
.IR SEAT|NAME .
A seat id is unambiguous anywhere; a name only means something inside one
session.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-hub " URL"
The hub. Defaults to
.I http://127.0.0.1:7870
and is usually taken from an identity file instead.
.RB [ NEXMESH_HUB_HTTP ]
.TP
.BI \-\-token " TOKEN"
Operator token. Mint one with
.BR "nexmesh-hub operator-token" .
.RB [ NEXMESH_OPERATOR_TOKEN ]
.TP
.BI \-\-ca\-cert " FILE"
PEM holding the hub's certificate, or the CA that signed it, for a
self\-signed hub.
.RB [ NEXMESH_CA_CERT ]
.TP
.BI \-\-client\-cert " FILE" ", \-\-client\-key " FILE
Your certificate and key, where the hub requires mutual TLS. Its name must
match the operator label the token was minted for.
.RB [ NEXMESH_CLIENT_CERT ", " NEXMESH_CLIENT_KEY ]
.TP
.BI \-\-identity\-dir " DIR"
A paired identity: anything not given explicitly is read from here. Defaults
to
.IR ~/.nexmesh .
.RB [ NEXMESH_IDENTITY ]
.SH THE CONSOLE
Run from a terminal with no command,
.B nexmesh
opens the operator console: the whole fleet on one screen, reading the same
snapshot the dashboard does. Panes for the deck, agents, chat, plan, memory,
the code graph, file locks and machines; a detail panel on the right; and the
code graph drawn in braille, subsystems and the dependencies between them.
.PP
Keys:
.BR 1 \(en 8
go to a pane,
.B Tab
cycles,
.B j
and
.B k
move,
.B a
or
.B Enter
attaches to the agent under the cursor,
.B i
types in the chat,
.B [
and
.B ]
change channel,
.B w
is the next workspace in the graph,
.B s
changes what the poll covers,
.B p
hides the detail panel,
.B t
switches theme,
.B r
refreshes,
.B ?
lists the keys and
.B q
quits.
.PP
Two themes, remembered in
.IR ~/.nexmesh/console.toml :
.B deck
is quiet and modern,
.B lcars
is not.
.PP
From a script or a pipe there is no console: it prints help and exits
non-zero, because a program that clears a screen because it was called
without arguments is a program that gets wrapped in
.I </dev/null
for ever.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Looking
.TP
.B health
The hub's version, and whether it answers.
.TP
.B nodes
Every machine, whether it is connected, and which agent CLIs it has.
.TP
.B sessions
Every session, with how many seats each holds.
.TP
.BI status " SESSION"
Every seat in one session: role, machine, work state, tokens, summary, and the
process behind it \(em its status, pid and whether it has a terminal.
.B STATE
is the work and outlives the process doing it;
.B AGENT
is whether there is a process there at all. A seat reading
.I idle
with
.I disconnected
beside it cannot be attached to or spawned into until something takes the seat
again. An
.B IO
of
.I pty
can be attached to and typed at,
.I pipes
can be watched, and a blank means the node never started that process \(em it
connected on its own, so only its own shim reaches it.
.TP
.BI log " SESSION " [ \-\-follow ]
What the agents have said to each other, with delivery state.
.TP
.BI transcript " SESSION WHO"
What one agent's process actually printed. Outlives the process.
.TP
.B certs
Certificates the fleet CA has issued.
.SS Teams and seats
.TP
.BI new\-session " NAME " [ \-\-workspace " NAME" " \-\-node " NODE " \-\-path " DIR ]
Create a session, optionally with a directory on one machine. That directory
is how a CLI started there finds this team.
.TP
.BI add\-workspace " SESSION PATH " \-\-node " NODE"
Mount another directory into a session that already exists.
.TP
.BI add\-agent " SESSION " \-\-cli " CLI " \-\-name " NAME " [ \-\-node " NODE" ]
Add an empty seat. Nothing runs until
.B spawn.
.TP
.BI spawn " SESSION NAME " [ CLI ]
.TQ
.BI spawn " SEAT " [ CLI ]
Start an agent in a seat, on the machine that seat belongs to. An
interactive seat runs the CLI's real terminal under a pty, which is what
makes
.B attach
possible. Close the CLI in its own terminal first, or the seat ends up with
two processes in it.
.IP
.IP
.BI \-\-mode " headless|interactive"
decides how it runs. Left out, a seat spawned FROM A TERMINAL becomes
interactive \(em someone typing this wants something to talk to \(em while a
spawn from a script or the orchestrator leaves the seat's own mode alone, so a
team declared headless does not quietly change shape because something
restarted it. Passed explicitly it is kept, like the CLI is.
.IP
Naming a
.I CLI
starts that one instead of the seat's usual: a seat is a place on a team,
not a commitment to one program, and the work may be better done by codex
today than by claude yesterday. The change is kept, because the seat really
is running that CLI from then on. What each machine has is in
.BR "nexmesh nodes" ,
and what each seat runs is the CLI column of
.BR "nexmesh status" .
.RS
.PP
.EX
nexmesh spawn payments Reviewer
nexmesh spawn payments Reviewer opencode
nexmesh spawn sl-1a2b3c4d
.EE
.RE
.TP
.BI rename\-agent " SEAT|NAME NEW\-NAME " [ \-\-session " SESSION" ]
Rename a seat. A seat id works on its own; a name only means something
inside a session, so it needs
.BR \-\-session .
A name already used in that session is refused: two seats with one name
leaves neither addressable.
.RS
.PP
.EX
nexmesh rename-agent sl-1a2b3c4d Reviewer
nexmesh rename-agent Claude-zeiss-3 Reviewer --session payments
.EE
.RE
.TP
.BI set\-role " SESSION NAME ROLE " [ \-\-description " TEXT" ]
.TQ
.BI set\-role " SEAT ROLE " [ \-\-description " TEXT" ]
Change what a seat is for. A role is not decoration:
.B list_peers
and the capability router use it to decide who to ask, and the review state
machine uses it to decide who may approve whose work. A team's needs change
\(em the engineer seat becomes the reviewer seat when the work moves from
building to checking \(em and deleting the seat to change that would throw
away its history and its inbox with it.
.RS
.PP
.EX
nexmesh set-role payments Reviewer reviewer
nexmesh set-role sl-1a2b3c4d architect --description "you decide the shape"
.EE
.RE
.TP
.BI rename\-session " SESSION NEW\-NAME"
Rename a team. Changes nothing else.
.TP
.BI move\-agent " SEAT SESSION"
Move a seat, and whoever is sitting in it, to another team. No restart. Its
workspace, file locks and ownership zones are released, because all three
describe the team being left. A conversation returns to the team it was
moved to on every later restart, whatever directory it starts in.
.RS
.PP
.EX
nexmesh move-agent sl-1a2b3c4d docs-site
.EE
.RE
.TP
.BI remove\-agent " SEAT " [ \-\-force ]
Remove a seat and the history that only describes who sat in it. Refuses
while somebody is sitting in it unless
.B \-\-force
is given.
.RS
.PP
.EX
nexmesh remove-agent sl-1a2b3c4d --force
.EE
.RE
.TP
.BI remove\-session " SESSION " [ \-\-force ]
Remove a session and everything that belonged to it: its seats and their
agent rows, its messages, workspaces, plan, knowledge, locks, capability
requests and the tokens scoped to it. Refuses while anybody is still sitting
in it unless
.B \-\-force
is given.
.SS Driving
.TP
.BI send " SESSION TEXT " [ \-\-to " WHO" ]
Say something to a team, or to one of its agents, as the operator.
.TP
.BI pause " SESSION " [ WHO ]
Stop giving work to one agent, or to everybody. A turn already running
finishes.
.TP
.BI resume " SESSION " [ WHO ]
Start again.
.TP
.BI release " SESSION " [ WHO ]
Accept the work and let the agent stop. Omit
.I WHO
to release the team.
.TP
.BI budget " SESSION " [ \-\-tokens " N" ]
Set, raise or clear a session's token ceiling. Changing it re\-arms the
guard, so a session paused on budget can be given more and resumed.
.TP
.BI sync " SESSION " \-\-op " publish|refresh|status"
Publish, pull, or report on a shared workspace, on every machine at once.
.SS Terminals
.TP
.BI attach " SESSION NAME|SEAT"
Attach to an agent's terminal, wherever it runs. Only reaches an agent the
hub holds a pty for: one it spawned in interactive mode, or one adopted from
tmux. A CLI you started by hand in an ordinary terminal has none, so
.B spawn
or
.B adopt
it first.
.RS
.PP
.EX
nexmesh attach payments Reviewer
.EE
.RE
.TP
.BI adopt " SESSION " \-\-node " NODE " \-\-tmux " TARGET " \-\-name " NAME"
Adopt a tmux session that is already running, so it can be watched and
driven from anywhere. Your own terminal stays attached.
.I TARGET
is a tmux session, window or pane \(em
.IR mywork ", " mywork:1.0 ", " %3 .
Adopting the same target twice returns the seat it had before rather than
making a second one.
.TP
.BI seat " SESSION NAME|SEAT " [ \-\-tmux " NAME" ] [ \-\-cmd " CMD" ]
Give one of your own conversations a keyboard: make the tmux session in the
directory that conversation was running in, adopt it, and start the seat's
own CLI inside it resuming the conversation that seat holds. A CLI started by
hand in an ordinary terminal can only be reached when it next calls a tool;
in an adopted pane the hub types at it in seconds.
.RS
.PP
Run it on the machine the seat belongs to \(em tmux is local, and so is the
node that adopts it. It refuses while that conversation is still running
somewhere else, since two CLIs on one conversation loses half of it, and it
types into the pane only when the pane is at a shell prompt.
.I \-\-cmd
says what to run instead of the default;
.I \-\-cmd
with an empty string makes the terminal and adopts it without starting
anything.
.I \-\-dry\-run
prints the three steps instead of taking them.
.PP
.EX
nexmesh seat payments Reviewer
.EE
.RE
.SS Joining a fleet
.TP
.BI pair " CODE"
Join as an operator using a one\-time code from the dashboard. The key is
generated here and never leaves this machine. Writes
.IR ~/.nexmesh/identity.json ,
after which every command above works with no options.
.TP
.BI install\-seat " " \-\-cli " CLI " [ \-\-role " ROLE" ]
Register the shim in one of your own agent CLIs, so every conversation you
start in it takes a seat. Deliberately takes no session: that configuration
is shared by every conversation on the machine, and a session named there
would pull them all onto one team. Place each conversation with
.B move\-agent
instead \(em it stays placed.
.TP
.BI install\-orchestrator " " \-\-cli " CLI " [ \-\-token " TOKEN" ]
Register
.BR nexmesh-orch (1)
in a CLI, so you can drive the fleet from the conversation you are already
in.
.TP
.BI revoke " NAME"
Revoke a machine or an operator. Its certificate stops working at the TLS
handshake, its token is revoked, and any connection it holds is closed.
.SH WHICH SESSION A CLI JOINS
A CLI started by hand has to end up somewhere. The hub decides in this
order:
.IP 1. 4
.BR NEXMESH_SESSION ,
which takes a session name or id. Exporting it for one launch
.RI ( "NEXMESH_SESSION=payments claude" )
decides that one conversation and nothing else.
.IP 2. 4
The seat that conversation already holds, wherever it was last placed.
.IP 3. 4
The directory, if a session has a workspace mounted at or above it
.I on this machine.
Only mounts on this node count; the longest match wins. Two sessions may
mount one directory, which makes the directory unable to answer rather than
forbidden.
.IP 4. 4
The only active session, if there is exactly one.
.IP 5. 4
Otherwise it refuses, and names the candidates.
.PP
A directory is a default, never a verdict: two agents in one folder may
belong to different teams, and one team's seats may sit in a dozen folders
on half a dozen machines.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.nexmesh/identity.json
A paired operator identity: hub address, token, and the names of the
certificate and key beside it. Mode 0600. Written by
.BR pair .
.TP
.I ~/.nexmesh/pki/
Certificates and keys for that identity.
.TP
.I /etc/nexmesh/clis/
CLI descriptors: how to start each agent CLI, how to give it the shim, and
how to drive it.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NEXMESH_HUB_HTTP
Hub URL.
.TP
.B NEXMESH_OPERATOR_TOKEN
Operator token.
.TP
.B NEXMESH_IDENTITY
Directory holding a paired identity.
.TP
.B NEXMESH_SESSION
Read by the MCP shim, not by this command: the session a CLI started in this
environment should join.
.TP
.B NEXMESH_CLI_SESSION
Pins a conversation identity for a CLI that publishes none, so every
reconnect reclaims the same seat.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Watch a team, then say something to one of its agents:
.PP
.RS
.nf
nexmesh status payments
nexmesh send payments "the loader is ready for review" --to Reviewer
.fi
.RE
.PP
Make the CLI you already use a member of a team, then place that one
conversation:
.PP
.RS
.nf
nexmesh install-seat --cli claude --role engineer
nexmesh status orchestrator            # find the seat it took
nexmesh move-agent sl-1a2b3c4d payments # it stays there from now on
.fi
.RE
.PP
Make a conversation attachable from anywhere. The tmux session is called
.IR mywork ,
the team
.IR payments ,
the seat
.IR Reviewer :
.PP
.RS
.nf
tmux new -s mywork                     # start claude inside it
nexmesh adopt payments --node machine-b --tmux mywork --name Reviewer
nexmesh attach payments Reviewer       # from any machine; detach freely
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR nexmesh-hub (1),
.BR nexmesh-node (1),
.BR nexmesh-orch (1),
.BR tmux (1)
.PP
The guide in
.I docs/GUIDE.md
covers the parts that do not fit here: lock domains, scoped and super
orchestrators, capability bridges, the code graph, and the dashboard.
